*Problem Definition*

*Hypothetical AI Problem:* Predicting Customer Churn for a Telecom Company

*Objectives:*

1. Identify customers at high risk of churning
2. Determine the most important factors contributing to customer churn
3. Develop a predictive model to inform retention strategies

*Stakeholders:*

1. Telecom Company Executives
2. Customer Service Managers

*KPI:* Customer retention rate

*Data Collection & Preprocessing*

*Data Sources:*

1. Customer demographic data (age, location, etc.)
2. Call records and billing data

*Potential Bias:* Selection bias due to incomplete demographic data

*Preprocessing Steps:*

1. Handling missing values using imputation or interpolation
2. Normalizing/scaling numerical features (e.g., call duration, bill amount)
3. Encoding categorical variables (e.g., customer plan type)

*Model Development*

*Model:* Random Forest Classifier

*Justification:* Handles high-dimensional data, robust to outliers, and provides feature importance scores

*Data Split:*

- Training set (70%): Used for model training
- Validation set (15%): Used for hyperparameter tuning
- Test set (15%): Used for final model evaluation

*Hyperparameters to Tune:*

1. Number of estimators (trees): Controls model complexity and accuracy
2. Maximum depth: Controls model complexity and risk of overfitting

*Evaluation & Deployment*

*Evaluation Metrics:*

1. Accuracy: Measures overall model performance
2. AUC-ROC: Measures model's ability to distinguish between churn and non-churn customers

*Concept Drift:* Changes in customer behavior or market conditions that can affect model performance over time

*Monitoring Concept Drift:* Regularly update the model with new data and monitor performance metrics (e.g., accuracy, AUC-ROC)

*Technical Challenge:* Scalability - handling large volumes of customer data and ensuring model predictions are generated in real-time to inform customer service interactions.
